note: 
  --> filter-positive.wipple:1:11
  |
1 | letters : "a", "b"
  |           --------

`"a", "b"` is a collection of `String` elements.

Since the first element, `"a"`, is a `String`, the remaining elements must also be a `String`.

This means `"a"` must have the same type as `"b"`.

note: 
  --> filter-positive.wipple:3:2
  |
3 | (filter positive?) letters
  |  ------

`filter` keeps the elements in `letters` that satisfy `positive?`.

This means `"a"` must have the same type as `n`.

This means `"b"` must have the same type as `n`.

note: 
  --> filter-positive.wipple:2:18
  |
2 | positive? : n -> n > 0
  |                  -----

`n > 0` returns `True` if `n` is greater than `0`.

Since `n` and `0` are compared, they must have the same type.

This means `n` must be a `Number`, like `0`.

error: 
  --> filter-positive.wipple:2:13
  |
2 | positive? : n -> n > 0
  |             ^

`n` is a `String` or a `Number`, but it can only be one of these.

